# Shapes: scale_shape

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

diamonds = Giggly.Datasets.diamonds()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_shape()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_shape.html), which covers
`scale_shape_binned()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code
first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what
it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`scale_shape()` maps a discrete column to six shapes that are easy to tell apart, solid by default
or hollow; a seventh value and any after it are not drawn, with a warning, and
`scale_shape_manual()` gives shapes by hand. A continuous column can be mapped to shapes only
through `scale_shape_binned()`, which cuts it into bins first, and since shapes have no order even
that is not advised. A shape can be named by number or by name, and only shapes 21 to 25 take a
fill. Giggly has `Giggly.Plot.scale_shape_discrete/2`, with its `solid:`, and
`Giggly.Plot.scale_shape_manual/2`, a shape named by number or by name as an atom, but not ggplot2's
short name, `scale_shape()`, nor binned or identity shape scales (`scale_shape_binned()`,
`scale_shape_identity()`), and by default maps `shape` to a discrete column as ggplot2's default
scale does, refusing a seventh value, and draws the legend that reads it back. A plot is described
with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell
aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot
and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. It binds ggplot2's `diamonds`,
`Giggly.Datasets.diamonds/0`, to `diamonds`, ggplot2's name for it.

## A shape for each cut

The examples draw a random sample of the `diamonds` dataset, picked after `set.seed(596)`.

```r
set.seed(596)
dsmall <- diamonds[sample(nrow(diamonds), 100), ]

(d <- ggplot(dsmall, aes(carat, price)) + geom_point(aes(shape = cut)))
d + scale_shape(solid = TRUE) # the default
d + scale_shape(solid = FALSE)
d + scale_shape(name = "Cut of diamond")
```

`name` titles the legend. `scale_shape()` is `Giggly.Plot.scale_shape_discrete/2`, which takes
`solid:` and `name:`. R's `sample()` picks the rows at random, the same ones every time after
`set.seed(596)`. The cell writes out the 100 row numbers it picks, counted from 1 as R counts rows,
and takes those rows of every column, as `diamonds[rows, ]` does. A `Giggly.Factor` enumerates as
its values alone, so `cut`, `color` and `clarity` are rebuilt with `Giggly.Factor.new/2`, keeping
their levels and their order, which give the cuts their shapes from Fair to Ideal.

```elixir
rows =
  ~w(25666 38189 6816 23077 19736 25700 11808 25189 26344 4071 45742 25297 52984 14612 48306
     19199 38194 37228 48850 49734 18386 14200 10993 24601 7011 34308 6583 38595 30236 1051 8079
     31287 1565 30482 44169 50061 13023 48715 16918 4754 43963 43370 2115 11166 9734 31412 31654
     45124 49949 51312 41254 39717 20773 13151 19964 12688 6155 31857 995 32150 46525 40773
     52088 46509 15601 45156 43334 43429 37708 30408 19060 32426 11867 24140 8179 51399 49507
     35248 49967 42223 29727 33565 41530 39904 49348 12696 15966 18558 8009 8575 13142 32231
     6305 39036 53530 34813 36021 47384 43849 23120)
  |> Enum.map(&String.to_integer/1)

dsmall =
  Enum.map(diamonds, fn {name, column} ->
    values = column |> Enum.to_list() |> List.to_tuple()
    taken = Enum.map(rows, fn row -> elem(values, row - 1) end)

    case column do
      %Giggly.Factor{levels: levels, ordered: ordered} ->
        {name, Giggly.Factor.new(taken, levels: levels, ordered: ordered)}

      _values ->
        {name, taken}
    end
  end)

plot =
  dsmall
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :carat, y: :price})
  |> Plot.geom_point(%{shape: :cut})

render.(plot)
```

```elixir
plot
|> Plot.scale_shape_discrete(solid: true)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
plot
|> Plot.scale_shape_discrete(solid: false)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
plot
|> Plot.scale_shape_discrete(name: "Cut of diamond")
|> render.()
```

## The order of the shapes

```r
# To change order of levels, change order of
# underlying factor
levels(dsmall$cut) <- c("Fair", "Good", "Very Good", "Premium", "Ideal")

# Need to recreate plot to pick up new data
ggplot(dsmall, aes(price, carat)) + geom_point(aes(shape = cut))
```

Shapes go to a column's values in the order of its levels. R's `levels<-` renames a factor's
levels in place, the first level to the first name and so on, and these are the names the levels
already have, so despite the comment the factor and its shapes are as before. Giggly gives a column
its levels with `Giggly.Factor.new/2`, whose `levels:` name the values each level holds, so the
same five levels leave it as it was too.

```elixir
cut =
  dsmall
  |> Keyword.fetch!(:cut)
  |> Enum.to_list()
  |> Giggly.Factor.new(levels: ["Fair", "Good", "Very Good", "Premium", "Ideal"], ordered: true)

dsmall = Keyword.replace!(dsmall, :cut, cut)

dsmall
|> Plot.new(%{x: :price, y: :carat})
|> Plot.geom_point(%{shape: :cut})
|> render.()
```

## The available shapes

```r
# Show a list of available shapes
df_shapes <- data.frame(shape = 0:24)
ggplot(df_shapes, aes(0, 0, shape = shape)) +
  geom_point(aes(shape = shape), size = 5, fill = 'red') +
  scale_shape_identity() +
  facet_wrap(~shape) +
  theme_void()
```

`scale_shape_identity()` draws each point in the shape its number names, and `facet_wrap()`
gives each a panel of its own. Giggly has `facet_wrap()`: `facet_wrap(~shape)` is
`Giggly.Plot.facet_wrap(plot, :shape)`.

Not in Giggly yet: `scale_shape_identity()` and `theme_void()`.